## Blocking issues

### P01 — Late canonical source drift is skipped for an originally absent restore target

`tool/reconcile_comment_counts.mjs:1023` returns `{remove:true}` for `!item.before.exists` before checking the canonical comment's current version. Restore admission checks current source heads once, but same-owner bounded continuation does not repeat that entire recovery proof. A later comment edit after recovery/later inverses can therefore pass immediately before a created contribution is deleted.

Reproduction: apply a baseline for an episode with one published comment; restore with maxItems 1 until the original created-contribution apply unit is next, after the later union inverse; change the canonical comment version while preserving published status; invoke the next restore step. Expected: reject without an owned inverse or checkpoint advance. Actual: the call succeeds and removes the created contribution. The probe fails `assert.rejects` with ERR_ASSERTION/Missing expected rejection, with one test executed and zero skips. It does not claim canonical content deletion, counter loss or an active unsafe release: the demonstrated violation is conditional restore proceeding despite fresh source drift while held.

Required correction: validate every applicable canonical source identity/version/contribution before performing an inverse, including removal where the owned preimage was absent. An absence of owned preimage is not an exemption from source CAS. Retain native conversion and no-resurrection behavior. Add the permanent regression before the fix; include present/missing/edited canonical comment variants and unchanged-source positive controls.

### P02 — Same-body native target-head ABA is adopted as owned proof

`effectiveHead` at lines 483–492 validates the current target against its referenced receipt, but never checks that the mutable head snapshot's native updateTime equals that receipt's commit version. `verifyHeadsStep` at lines 908–915 compares only the frozen head body during ancestry. Rewriting a genuine head through another value and back to its exact original body changes its native version without being rejected; full apply can then reach active.

Reproduction: stage an episode and enter verifying; rewrite one actual target head with a transient field, then its exact original body; assert native updateTime changed; complete apply. Expected: reject unowned native head version. Actual: active completes. The separate probe executes one business assertion and fails Missing expected rejection, zero skips. All normal owned head writes occur atomically with the head's currentReceipt, providing the exact native seal without a new schema/service.

Required correction: bind effective/frozen/resumed current head metadata to the immutable receipt that owns its current physical image, checking schema and native identity before trusting it. Body equality alone cannot authorize an ABA. Preserve no-op target observed versions: the HEAD itself still has the new receipt commit version even when its TARGET is unchanged. Add permanent same-body overwrite/delete-recreate and no-op/restore-head positive controls before the fix. This is separate from the security review's unknown-field/purpose-schema issue.

### P03 — New exported apply/restore APIs accept an unapproved clock override

`fullContext` at lines 498–503 destructures optional `now=Date.now` from caller options and propagates it into lease ownership/recovery. The approved injected clock exception belongs only to the private QA child process seam; it does not authorize a new tool/application API parameter. Existing early bootstrap/store clock compatibility is distinct and must be preserved. The security reviewer separately reproduced owner takeover before real lease expiry using this parameter; see that independent report/probe.

Required correction: remove/reject the clock override from the new APIs and keep normal runtime lease time authoritative. Any deterministic QA clock mechanism must remain confined to the approved private process boundary, without a production flag or public API bypass. Regression tests must prove another owner cannot advance time through exported arguments, while existing bootstrap callers/default sender contracts remain unchanged.

